  export const questions = [
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'Explain closures with a practical example.',a:'A closure is created when a function retains access to variables from its lexical scope, even when called elsewhere or after the outer function has returned. Common uses include private state, callbacks, factories, and memoization. Be ready to code a counter and explain what remains in memory.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'What is hoisting and what is the temporal dead zone?',a:'Declarations are processed before a scope executes, but they behave differently. Function declarations can be called before their textual position; var is hoisted and initialised to undefined; let and const are in scope but cannot be accessed before initialisation, creating the temporal dead zone.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'What is the difference between == and ===?',a:'== permits type coercion before comparing; === compares without coercing types. Prefer === in normal application code because its behaviour is more predictable, and understand the few cases where coercion changes the result.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'Primitive values vs reference values: what is copied?',a:'Primitives are copied as values. Objects and arrays are assigned by reference value, so two variables may refer to the same object. A shallow copy creates a new outer object but nested objects can still be shared.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'When should you use map, filter, reduce, find, some, and every?',a:'Use map to transform each item; filter to keep matching items; reduce to accumulate into one result; find to return the first matching element; some to test whether at least one item matches; and every to test whether all items match.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'How does the event loop order Promise callbacks and setTimeout?',a:'Synchronous JavaScript runs first. Promise reactions and queueMicrotask callbacks enter the microtask queue, which is drained before the next timer/task runs (subject to the host environment). A common example prints synchronous logs, then Promise callbacks, then a zero-delay timer.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'Promise.all vs Promise.allSettled?',a:'Promise.all fulfils when every input fulfils and rejects as soon as an input rejects. Promise.allSettled waits for all inputs to settle and returns an outcome object for each one. Use all when all results are needed to continue; use allSettled when you need every success/failure outcome.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'What is the difference between debounce and throttle?',a:'Debounce waits until events have stopped for a specified interval before running, which is useful for search input. Throttle limits execution to at most a chosen frequency, which can help during scroll or resize events.'},
    {cat:'JavaScript',q:'What are call, apply, and bind?',a:'They let you set the this value for a function. call invokes immediately with arguments individually; apply invokes immediately with an array-like arguments value; bind returns a new function with this (and optionally leading arguments) preset.'},
    {cat:'React',q:'What causes a React component to re-render?',a:'A component renders initially and can render again when its state updates, when its parent renders, or when a context value it consumes changes. A render is React calculating UI; it does not necessarily mean the DOM changes. Memoization can skip some renders when its conditions are met.'},
    {cat:'React',q:'Does changing a normal variable or a ref trigger a re-render?',a:'No. Changing a regular local variable does not schedule rendering, and changing ref.current does not schedule rendering either. Use state for values that determine UI; refs are useful for mutable values or DOM nodes that do not themselves need to trigger a render.'},
    {cat:'React',q:'Why are keys important? Why can array indexes be a problem?',a:'Keys help React match siblings between renders. Prefer stable IDs. If an item is inserted, deleted, or reordered, index keys can cause state or DOM to be associated with the wrong item because positions change. Index keys are reasonable for truly static lists that never reorder or change.'},
    {cat:'React',q:'Why can state appear unchanged immediately after a setter?',a:'State behaves as a snapshot for the currently executing render. Calling a setter queues a future render rather than changing the variable in the existing event handler. Use a functional updater such as setCount(c => c + 1) when the next value depends on prior state.'},
    {cat:'React',q:'What is reconciliation?',a:'React compares the next element tree with the current one to determine what should change. Element type, position, and keys help React preserve or reset component identity. Reconciliation is not the same as saying that every render causes a full DOM rebuild.'},
    {cat:'Hooks & performance',q:'When should you use useEffect?',a:'Use an Effect to synchronise with an external system, such as a network connection, browser API, subscription, or non-React widget. Do not add effects just to derive one piece of render data from another; calculate derived values during rendering where appropriate.'},
    {cat:'Hooks & performance',q:'useEffect cleanup: when does it run?',a:'Cleanup runs before the Effect runs again with changed dependencies and when the component unmounts. In development Strict Mode, React may perform an extra setup/cleanup cycle to reveal unsafe effects. Use cleanup to unsubscribe, disconnect, or cancel/ignore obsolete work.'},
    {cat:'Hooks & performance',q:'useRef vs useState?',a:'State updates schedule a render, so use it for data displayed in the UI. Ref updates do not schedule a render, so use refs for DOM elements, timer IDs, or mutable values that need to persist across renders but are not themselves render output.'},
    {cat:'Hooks & performance',q:'When are useMemo, useCallback, and React.memo useful?',a:'useMemo caches a calculated value; useCallback caches a function identity; React.memo can skip a child render when props compare equal. They are optimisation tools, not correctness requirements. Profile first and consider the cost and whether memoisation actually avoids meaningful work.'},
    {cat:'Hooks & performance',q:'How do you prevent stale API responses from replacing new results?',a:'Cancel obsolete fetches with AbortController where possible or track the identity of the latest request and ignore older responses. Make sure the effect cleanup and dependencies reflect the values that determine the request.'},
    {cat:'Hooks & performance',q:'Context API vs prop drilling vs a state library?',a:'Props make data flow explicit for local component trees. Context can distribute values to many consumers without threading props through every level, but changing context can update its consumers. A dedicated state library may help when shared state, workflows, debugging, or caching needs justify the extra abstraction.'},
    {cat:'Project & experience',q:'Tell me about yourself for a React developer role.',a:'Use a concise structure: overall frontend experience; your starting point in Angular; the React work you do today; a couple of relevant strengths or examples; and the kind of React role you are targeting. Keep every claim grounded in your actual work and be precise about how much hands-on React experience you have.'},
    {cat:'Project & experience',q:'Walk me through a project you worked on.',a:'Explain the product and users first, then your responsibility, component architecture, data flow and API integration. Choose one meaningful technical decision, one challenge and its outcome. Be clear about what you personally implemented versus what the team owned.'},
    {cat:'Project & experience',q:'Describe a production bug or difficult issue you solved.',a:'Use situation, investigation, root cause, action, and result. Mention how you reproduced the issue, what evidence narrowed the cause, how you verified the fix, and any test or monitoring that prevented recurrence. Use a real story and quantify the result only when you can support it.'},
    {cat:'Project & experience',q:'Why are you moving toward a React-focused role?',a:'Connect your existing frontend experience to the work you now do in React. Explain what you enjoy about building user interfaces, how you are deepening your React skills, and why the role fits the direction you want to grow. Avoid portraying Angular as a weakness; focus on transferable concepts and real React examples.'}
  ,
    {cat:"JavaScript",lvl:"Advanced",q:"How does prototypal inheritance work, and what does class syntax do?",a:"Every object has an internal prototype link; property lookup walks the chain until it finds the key or reaches null. class is syntax over constructor functions: methods live on Class.prototype, extends links prototypes, and super calls the parent. Know Object.create and Object.hasOwn."},
    {cat:"JavaScript",q:"How is this determined, and how do arrow functions differ?",a:"For normal functions this depends on the call site: obj.method(), a plain call (undefined in strict mode), new, or explicit call/apply/bind. Arrow functions have no own this and capture it lexically, which suits callbacks but not object methods that need dynamic this."},
    {cat:"JavaScript",lvl:"Advanced",q:"What are currying and partial application, and when are they useful?",a:"Currying turns f(a,b,c) into f(a)(b)(c); partial application pre-fills some arguments. Both help build configured, reusable functions and compose behaviour. Be ready to implement curry using fn.length."},
    {cat:"JavaScript",q:"Shallow vs deep copy: how do you clone safely?",a:"Spread and Object.assign copy one level only. structuredClone handles nested data, Dates, Maps, Sets and cycles, but not functions or DOM nodes. JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x)) drops undefined and functions and turns Dates into strings. Interviewers may ask for a recursive clone."},
    {cat:"JavaScript",q:"When does || go wrong compared with ?? and ?.",a:"|| falls back for any falsy value, so 0, empty string and false get replaced, a classic bug with counts and flags. ?? falls back only for null or undefined. ?. short-circuits to undefined when the left side is null or undefined."},
    {cat:"JavaScript",q:"ES modules vs CommonJS: what is tree shaking?",a:"ESM uses static import/export, so bundlers can see which exports are unused and drop them (tree shaking). CommonJS require is dynamic and runs at runtime. ESM also gives live bindings and top-level await."},
    {cat:"React",q:"Controlled vs uncontrolled components: when do you use each?",a:"A controlled input takes its value from state and updates through onChange, giving instant validation and formatting. An uncontrolled input keeps its value in the DOM and is read via a ref or FormData on submit, which is simpler for basic forms and file inputs."},
    {cat:"React",lvl:"Advanced",q:"What are error boundaries and what do they not catch?",a:"A class component with getDerivedStateFromError or componentDidCatch that renders fallback UI when a child throws during rendering. They do not catch event-handler errors, async code, server rendering, or errors in the boundary itself. react-error-boundary is the usual wrapper."},
    {cat:"React",lvl:"Advanced",q:"What are portals, and how do refs reach a child DOM node?",a:"createPortal renders children into another DOM node, such as document.body, while keeping React tree behaviour and event bubbling. It suits modals and tooltips that must escape overflow or z-index. Refs reach children through forwardRef, or as a normal prop in newer React versions."},
    {cat:"React",lvl:"Advanced",q:"What changed with automatic batching and concurrent features in React 18?",a:"State updates are batched everywhere, including promises and timeouts, not just inside event handlers. startTransition and useTransition mark updates as non-urgent so typing stays responsive; useDeferredValue defers a value; Suspense coordinates loading states."},
    {cat:"React",q:"You know Angular: how does React differ conceptually?",a:"Angular is a full framework with templates, dependency injection, RxJS and zone-based change detection. React is a UI library with JSX, one-way data flow, hooks and explicit state updates, so you choose routing and data tools yourself. Frame it as an ecosystem choice, not a verdict."},
    {cat:"Hooks & performance",q:"useState vs useReducer: when do you switch?",a:"Use useReducer when state has several related fields, transitions follow named actions, or the next state depends on complex rules. It centralises logic, is easy to test as a pure function, and pairs well with Context for shared state."},
    {cat:"Hooks & performance",q:"What are the Rules of Hooks, and what makes a good custom hook?",a:"Call hooks only at the top level of components or other hooks, never in loops, conditions or nested functions, so React can match them by call order. A good custom hook has one responsibility, a clear return shape, and cleans up what it starts."},
    {cat:"Hooks & performance",lvl:"Advanced",q:"How do you debug an effect that loops forever or reads stale values?",a:"Loops usually come from object, array or function dependencies recreated every render, or setState inside an effect without a guard. Stale values come from missing dependencies. Fixes: functional updaters, move values inside the effect, stabilise references only when needed, or keep the latest value in a ref."},
    {cat:"Hooks & performance",q:"useEffect vs useLayoutEffect?",a:"useEffect runs after the browser paints; useLayoutEffect runs after DOM updates but before paint, blocking it. Use useLayoutEffect only to measure or adjust layout and avoid flicker; default to useEffect."},
    {cat:"Hooks & performance",q:"How would you render 10,000 rows efficiently?",a:"Virtualise (react-window or similar) so only visible rows mount, or paginate. Keep stable keys, memoise row components, avoid heavy work in render, and move filtering and sorting server-side if the data set is large."},
    {cat:"Hooks & performance",q:"What is code splitting with React.lazy and Suspense?",a:"React.lazy loads a component through dynamic import() only when it first renders, and Suspense shows a fallback meanwhile. Split by route or heavy features, handle load failures with an error boundary, and verify gains in the bundle analyser."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",lvl:"Advanced",q:"Server state vs client state: when would you use TanStack Query or Redux Toolkit?",a:"Server state is remote data that goes stale and needs caching, refetching, deduping and retries, which TanStack Query handles. Client state such as UI flags or wizard steps suits useState, Context, Zustand or Redux Toolkit when many distant components share it with complex updates."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",q:"How do you test a React component?",a:"With React Testing Library, render the component and interact the way a user does: query by role, label and text rather than implementation details, then assert visible outcomes. Mock network at the boundary (for example with MSW) and test hooks through components."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",q:"CSR vs SSR vs SSG: what is hydration?",a:"CSR renders in the browser; SSR renders HTML per request; SSG builds HTML ahead of time. Hydration attaches React event handlers to server-rendered HTML. Trade-offs are first paint, SEO, server cost and freshness. Mismatches between server and client output cause hydration errors."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",lvl:"Advanced",q:"How would you build an accessible modal or dropdown?",a:"Use semantic roles and aria attributes (role=dialog, aria-modal, labels), move focus in on open and restore it on close, trap Tab inside a modal, close on Escape and outside click, lock background scroll, and render through a portal."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",q:"How do you protect a React app from XSS and handle tokens?",a:"React escapes interpolated values by default; avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML unless the HTML is sanitised. Prefer httpOnly, secure cookies for session tokens over localStorage, add CSRF protection when using cookies, and always validate on the server."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",q:"How would you structure a large React codebase?",a:"Group by feature (components, hooks, API calls and tests colocated), keep a small shared UI and utilities layer, use consistent naming, TypeScript, linting and absolute imports, and avoid deep cross-feature imports that create hidden coupling."},
    {cat:"Architecture & testing",lvl:"Advanced",q:"Frontend system design: how do you approach building a news feed or autocomplete?",a:"Use a structure such as RADIO: Requirements, Architecture (component tree), Data model and state, Interface (API, pagination, errors), Optimisations (caching, virtualisation, debouncing, accessibility). State assumptions and trade-offs out loud."},
    {cat:"Project & experience",q:"Tell me about a disagreement with a teammate or reviewer.",a:"Pick a real, low-drama example. Describe both positions fairly, how you used evidence (benchmarks, user impact, a prototype) to decide, and what you learned. Show you can disagree and still commit."},
    {cat:"Project & experience",q:"How do you break down and estimate a feature with unclear requirements?",a:"Clarify the goal and users first, list assumptions and open questions, slice the work into thin vertical increments, call out risks and dependencies, give a range rather than a single number, and share progress early."},
    {cat:"Project & experience",q:"What questions would you ask the interviewer?",a:"Ask about how the team ships and reviews code, how they handle tech debt and testing, what success looks like in the first 90 days, and the biggest frontend challenge right now. Avoid questions answered on the careers page."}
  ];

